## The display, and what to do if you get no picture

This board does not route the HDMI DDC lines through to the display controller, so the
kernel can never read your TV's EDID and cannot pick a mode for you. The image forces one
instead, currently `video=HDMI-A-1:1920x1080@60` in `/boot/armbianEnv.txt`.

If your display cannot show 1080p, you get no signal. Fix it over SSH:

```bash
sudo h96-display safe      # 1280x720@60, the mode every HDMI display must accept
sudo reboot
sudo h96-display keep      # run this once you can SEE the picture
```

`keep` is not optional. Without it the mode reverts on the next boot, which is exactly
what saves you if you set a mode your display cannot show. Other modes are `1080p`,
`1440p`, `4k`, or any size you like, for example `sudo h96-display 1440x900` or
`sudo h96-display 2560x1440@60`.

If you have no picture and no SSH, mount the boot partition on another machine and edit
the `video=` line by hand. See `/boot/h96-display-README.txt`.

The same missing EDID is why the image names a substitute one on the kernel command line.
The substitute named in v3.3 was one of the kernel's **built-in** EDID blobs, which loads
without needing any file. It declared no audio capability, so the HDMI output dropped into
a picture-only mode and sound never reached the TV.

v4.0 names a file instead, and measuring a fresh flash showed that file is never actually
loaded: the display driver starts before the root filesystem is in use, so it cannot read
it. The outcome is no EDID at all, and that is the case the driver handles most
permissively, assuming a normal HDMI sink with audio. Sound works. The argument itself is
doing nothing, which is why it is left alone rather than "corrected" into a built-in name
that would break audio again.

