## Known limitations

- **The display cannot be auto-detected.** A mode is forced in `/boot/armbianEnv.txt`.
  See the display section above.
- **Ethernet link can take minutes to come up.** The RTL8211F PHY may spend a long time
  attempting Gigabit auto-negotiation before downshifting to 100Mbps, and the link is
  unusable until it does. Measured at 26 s, 128 s and 190 s across boots on one unit. This
  is below the OS, not boot time. Try another cable or switch port first; to skip Gigabit
  negotiation at the cost of capping the port:
  `ethtool -s eth0 speed 100 duplex full autoneg off`.
- **WiFi does not use NetworkManager.** This chip cannot complete its association through
  NetworkManager, so `wlan0` is driven by a standalone `wpa_supplicant`. Use
  `h96-wifi-connect` or the tray icon, not `nmcli`.
- **Bluetooth headset microphones never work.** The BSP kernel has no `rfcomm` module, so
  HFP and HSP cannot function. A2DP audio out is unaffected.
- **In-browser video decode stays on the CPU.** Ubuntu's Chromium is built without the
  V4L2 decoder this hardware needs. That is what the `ff2mpv` button is for.
- **CPU voltage cannot be set from userspace.** `/sys/class/regulator/.../microvolts` is
  read-only in the kernel by design, so `h96-bench --tune` and `--set-volt` do not work.
  Voltage lives in the device tree. The opt-in `h96-undervolt` tool changes it there
  instead, per chip, and reverts itself unless you confirm it. It is opt-in and ships
  inert; `UNDERVOLT_README.md` in the release download explains it.
- **Big cores top out at 2208 MHz.** That is where the vendor OPP table ends.
- **Anti-cheat games never work** under x86-on-arm64 emulation, and the Steam snap does
  not work on this GPU stack.

